<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="153" project="spaarke-ontology-platform-r1">
  <metadata>
    <title>A Do item closed because someone satisfied it writes a Routine Decision Record naming them (D-145b)</title>
    <phase>4 Decision Record</phase>
    <gate>blocked: #1907 (task 149) merged into docs/ontology-platform-design (same writer file)</gate>
    <status>completed</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>New secure-family record write from the nightly job; extends the single Decision Record create path.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Adds a writer entry point under the D-34 single-create-site guard and secure-family ownership; attribution rules need judgment.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <deps>149</deps>
    <tags>bff-api,job,dataverse,security,testing</tags>
    <estimated-effort>4-6 hours</estimated-effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>When a Do item's underlying work is done outside the decision route (someone completes the To Do on its form, records the work-assignment response), the nightly pass closes the Signal as ConditionCleared with no Decision Record and with `sprk_resolvedby`/`sprk_confirmedby` null (`SignalWriter.cs:854` "a system closure has no Decision Record"; `SignalClosureService.cs:41-43`; called from `SignalReevaluationJob.cs:387`). The owner decided (D-145b): that closure is recorded, naming the person who did the work.</prompt>
  <role>SPAARKE BFF engineer.</role>
  <goal>Every Do-lane Signal the nightly pass closes as ConditionCleared gets exactly one Routine Decision Record that names, best effort, the user who changed the underlying record; nothing else about system closures changes.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>D-145, D-129, D-34, D-123, D-139 (notes/decisions.md). Task 149's `WriteGateDecisionAsync` is the nearest precedent: a non-commit entry point on `DecisionRecordWriter`, an idempotency key in `sprk_steps`, ownership through `RecordOwnershipResolver`, one CreateAsync site pinned by `DecisionRecordContextImmutabilityTests`. Re-read the merged 149 code at your base.</background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file>src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Signals/SignalClosureService.cs and SignalWriter.cs (ConditionCleared close)</file>
      <file>src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Signals/SignalReevaluationJob.cs (:387 caller)</file>
      <file>src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Signals/DecisionRecordWriter.cs (REFERENCE: WriteGateDecisionAsync from 149 — copy its shape)</file>
    </relevant-files>
  </context>

  <justification>
    <existing>DecisionRecordWriter (commit and gate entry points); SignalClosureService closes ConditionCleared Signals.</existing>
    <extension>Add one writer entry point and call it from the existing closure; no new service, route, registration or column.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>A Do item closed by a colleague leaves no record of who closed it or why, contrary to D-145b.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="D-145b">Scope: Do-lane Signals closed as ConditionCleared only. Superseded, PolicyRetired and Decide/Know-lane closures stay record-free, unchanged.</constraint>
    <constraint source="D-145b">Record class Routine; `sprk_action` null; the Signal links to the record (`sprk_decisionrecord`) and `sprk_resolvedby` is set when the person is known. Attribution, best effort, from the subject row the predicate read: the user in its `modifiedby` as read by the closing pass. If that user is an application user or the Spaarke service identity, or the read fails, write the record with `sprk_confirmedby` null and a reason saying the person is unknown; never guess, never use the assignee or the owner (D-129).</constraint>
    <constraint source="D-139 / 149">Exactly one record per Signal closure: deterministic idempotency key from the Signal id and its episode, stored the way 149 stores its key; a re-run of the nightly pass writes nothing new. The key must not collide with 149's gate key (different prefix) and 149's gate lookup must still match only gate records (it filters `sprk_confirmedby` Null plus its key; check both still hold).</constraint>
    <constraint source="D-34">One CreateAsync site in DecisionRecordWriter; the immutability ArchTests stay green, extended for the new entry point if needed, never weakened.</constraint>
    <constraint source="D-123 / D-33">Ownership via RecordOwnershipResolver exactly as the other entry points do; a Signal in a secure family produces a secure-child record. Logs carry counts and type names only.</constraint>
    <constraint source="D-61">No extra writes to unchanged Signals; the record write happens only on a real close.</constraint>
    <constraint source="fail-closed">A failure to write the record does not block or undo the close; it is logged and counted on `ontology.writer.failures` (the 035 alert sees it) and retried on the next pass through the idempotent key.</constraint>
    <constraint source="bff-extensions">Publish-size delta vs a fresh build of origin/master, no new HIGH CVE; report the added Dataverse requests per closed Signal.</constraint>
    <constraint source="D-123">uac-r2 reviews the PR (SendMessage + #1355).</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="1">Re-read the ConditionCleared close path and 149's gate entry point at your base.</step>
    <step order="2">Add the writer entry point and the attribution read; call it from the closure.</step>
    <step order="3">Tests (filtered), seeding proof on idempotency, size delta, PR into docs/ontology-platform-design.</step>
  </steps>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">A colleague completes Alice's To Do on its form; the nightly pass closes Alice's Do Signal and writes one Routine record with `sprk_confirmedby` = the colleague, linked to the Signal.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Running the pass again writes nothing new.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`modifiedby` is an application user or the read fails: one record with `sprk_confirmedby` null and the unknown-person reason.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative: Superseded, PolicyRetired and a Decide-lane ConditionCleared write no record.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A secure-family Do Signal's record is a secure child (owned as the other entry points own it).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A record-write failure leaves the Signal closed and is counted on `ontology.writer.failures`.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">149's gate idempotency tests and the single-create-site ArchTests stay green.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="false">Test scope: the cases above only; seeding proof on the idempotency key.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If attribution needs audit-history reads or a new column, STOP and report the options.</trigger>
    <trigger>If the change requires editing uac-r2's files, STOP and report.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <knowledge>
    <files>
      <file>.claude/constraints/jobs.md</file>
      <file>.claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md</file>
      <file>.claude/constraints/testing.md</file>
      <file>.claude/adr/ADR-052-workload-placement.md</file>
    </files>
  </knowledge>
  <notes>
    <note date="2026-10-15">Implemented on task/ontology-153-satisfied-close-dr (PR into docs/ontology-platform-design, awaiting review; not merged). Writer entry point WriteConditionClearedAsync (key "cleared-dr-{signal}-{episode}" in sprk_steps.reviewId, lookup also requires the step code "condition-cleared"); the gate is now named by Plan.IsGate, not by an empty confirmer. Attribution: subject modifiedby, only an Eligible person (ClassifyEligibility) whose change falls after the Signal's createdon and not after the close; otherwise confirmedby null + condition_cleared_person_unknown. Job: close, then record, then a conditional link (sprk_decisionrecord + sprk_resolvedby); Do lane only; re-file closes excluded; failures metered, run fails, retried for 7 days. No census entry needed (no owner write added). Judgment calls for review: re-file exclusion, the attribution window, the 7-day retry window (it also backfills Do ConditionCleared closures from the 7 days before deploy).</note>
    <note date="2026-10-15">Adversarial pass 1 fixed (PR #1953 "Adversarial pass 1"). F1: a permanently refused closure record (subject deleted: the attribution read by id finds no row, new refusal decision_cleared_subject_missing; SubjectUnfileable; SubjectUnsupported; a core type naming no core table) is a counted skip on ontology.writer.skips (tags cleared_dr_skipped_*), logged at Warning, marked in the distributed cache (sprk:ontology:evaluator:cleared-dr-skipped:{signal}:{episode}, retry window + 1 day) and never re-attempted; ClosureRecordFailures no longer fails the run (transient failures still meter ontology.writer.failures and are retried for 7 days). The job test World now faults a by-id read of a missing row with FaultException&lt;OrganizationServiceFault&gt; 0x80040217, as ServiceClient does. F2 (D-128 Q18): the link replaces exactly the record the Signal named when the job closed it (a reminder or inquiry), only while Resolved/ConditionCleared, under the row-version check; any other link is a skip (cleared_dr_skipped_foreign_link, Warning). Retries of a linked closure read the linked records once per policy per pass (chunks of 100) and retry only those whose record is not this closure's own and was created before the close. F3: Plan.NoRequester (set only in ValidateConditionCleared) makes OwnershipContextOf pass RequestedBy null, so sprk_createdbyperson stays empty; the person stays in sprk_confirmedby. K4: the job meters every exception except DecisionRecordRefusedException and SignalWriterEscalationException (an access denial from its own core-type or assignee read included). Gate lookup, Plan.IsGate and the condition-cleared lookup unchanged.</note>
    <note date="2026-10-15">Known limits (adversarial pass 1, documented, no code). K1: a person's later re-close overwrites the system's link (DecisionCommitService.cs:1946), so two records name one Signal; the human decision wins. K2: attribution is the subject's last modifier; flows, bulk imports and app-only BFF writes give that identity or "unknown"; impersonated writes attribute correctly. K3: a re-file closure whose new episode was suppressed can be backfilled with a "person unknown" record. Also: the skip marker lives in the distributed cache; if it is evicted, one more attempt is made and the skip is metered again (never a failure). CORRECTED in adversarial pass 2: that held for the writer's permanent refusals, NOT for a ForeignLink skip. With the marker evicted, the retry took any linked record created before the close for the D-128 link and overwrote it. The retry now replaces a prior link only when that record was created by the writer AND took only non-resolving actions (a reminder or an inquiry); any other prior link is foreign whatever the marker says.</note>
    <note date="2026-10-15">Adversarial pass 2 fixed (PR #1953 "Adversarial pass 2"). F1 (security): both "already recorded" lookups (149's FindGateRecordAsync and 153's FindConditionClearedRecordAsync) and the job's linked-record read (RetriesReplacingAPriorLinkAsync) now match only rows with createdby = the ontology writer's own systemuser. Writer identity: OntologyWriterDataverseClient.GetWriterUserIdAsync, WhoAmI on the writer's dedicated managed-identity connection, cached for the connection's life after the first answer (a fault or an empty id is never cached). Confirmed before choosing: the writer creates every Decision Record through _writerClient.CreateAsync with no impersonation (no CallerId anywhere in Services/Signals), so createdby is the writer; review records from the commit route are ALSO created by the writer, and stay excluded by the other conditions (the gate's sprk_confirmedby IS NULL; the condition-cleared step code). No fallback to "any application user" (uac-r2: that set includes the BFF's general app user). Fail closed: if WhoAmI faults, the lookup is refused (gate_record_lookup_failed / decision_cleared_lookup_failed, metered on ontology.writer.failures) and nothing is written; the job's linked-record read faults the same way and is metered by the job. uac-r2's SecureChildReconciler re-owns records (ownerid), which leaves createdby alone, so a re-owned record still matches. The gate lookup changed ONLY by the creator condition. F3: the foreign-link test now seeds the foreign record (writer-created reminder, created before the close), so its night-2 assertion depends on the skip marker. K (foreign link vs eviction): see the corrected note above. K1: a NotApplicable link outcome for a record this pass wrote (not already recorded) is a Warning plus a skip metered as cleared_dr_skipped_signal_reclosed. K (silent abandon): an unlinked ConditionCleared closure from an earlier pass, inside the retry window and still its subject's latest episode, whose subject is a hit again is logged at Warning (DecisionRecordEvents.ConditionClearedSkipped) on that pass; the next pass sees the new episode, so it is said once. Not metered.</note>
    <note date="2026-10-15">Known limits (adversarial pass 2, documented, no code). (1) Publish and the nightly run race: ReevaluatePolicyAsync has no lease, so a publish-triggered pass and the scheduled pass can both create a closure record for the same key (the lookup-then-create is not atomic); the 149 gate has the same race. (2) The skip-marker cache key (sprk:ontology:evaluator:cleared-dr-skipped:{signal}:{episode}) has no environment scope; Signal ids are GUIDs, so a collision across environments sharing one cache is not expected, but the key does not exclude it. (3) SubjectUnfileable (two conflicting roots) is marked permanent for 8 days (retry window + 1 day) even if the conflict is later fixed. (4) The per-night request cost of retries is about ceil(L/100) record reads per Do policy (L = linked ConditionCleared closures in the window), plus one WhoAmI per writer connection life. (5) The silent-abandon warning covers only the "subject is a hit again" exit; an unlinked closure that reaches the end of the 7-day window is still dropped silently (each failed attempt was already logged and metered), and a closure linked to a D-128 reminder is not covered (detecting it needs the linked-record read). (6) The first-pass replace rule (the link replaces the record the Signal named when the job closed it) does not apply the writer-and-D-128 test; only retries do.</note>
    <note date="2026-10-15">uac-r2 re-check of pass 2 fixed (PR #1953 "uac-r2 re-check fixes"). F1 (security, predates 153): DecisionCommitService.FindRecordAsync (the commit's "already written for this review" lookup) matched on sprk_confirmedby = caller and sprk_steps reviewId LIKE only, so a Console User or Ontology Administrator (Deep create on sprk_decisionrecord) could pre-create a row with their own confirmer, the review id, the predictable reviewHash and resolvedSignalIds, and the commit's finishing path would close the Signals with the forged resolution and run no executor. It now also requires createdby = OntologyWriterDataverseClient.GetWriterUserIdAsync (the same rule as the gate and closure lookups); a WhoAmI fault lands in the existing catch and refuses as Unavailable, never as "no record found". Every other condition is unchanged. Other sprk_decisionrecord reads in Services/Signals: DecisionRecordWriter.FindGateRecordAsync and FindConditionClearedRecordAsync and SignalReevaluationJob.RetriesReplacingAPriorLinkAsync (all already creator-guarded); SignalSuppressionService.ReadDecisionRecordsAsync (by record id, display facts only: class, reason, decidedon; no idempotency or "already recorded" decision); TodoAssigneeOwnerReconciliationJob reads Signals by their decision-record link (not a Decision Record lookup). None other has the flaw. K2: IsNonResolvingReviewRecord now takes sprk_followons as well and both it and DecisionCommitService.StoredRecord.RecordedResolution derive from one rule, DecisionRecordWriter.LeavesSignalOpen (something done, and no taken action resolves), so a review with only Next steps (InquirySent) is replaced on retry. The one remaining difference is deliberate: a taken code the catalog does not know makes the retry rule false. Every linked retry that is still not replaced (record not among the writer's, created after the close, resolving, unknown code) is now logged at Warning and metered as cleared_dr_skipped_unrecognised_prior_link; the closure's own record stays silent. Known limit K1 (documented, no code): the lookups match only records created by the CURRENT writer application user. If the writer's managed identity or app user is recreated, if data is restored into an environment with a different writer user, or if fixture rows were written under a developer credential, earlier gate, closure and review records stop matching: a repeated review would write a second record, and a gate or closure would be recorded again (duplicates; no data is lost or overwritten). Remedy: re-stamp is not possible (createdby is system-managed); accept the duplicates, or de-duplicate by sprk_steps reviewId after an identity change. No ontology alerts runbook exists in the repo yet, so the limit is recorded here and in the PR body.</note>
  </notes>
</task>
